% ============================================================================
% seed-parts — the parts of a seed and the defining role / function the source
% states for each one (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A seed is not one solid pellet: it is an assembly of named parts, and a seed-
% biology text names each part by the ONE thing it is or does. "The seedcoat is
% the seed-covering structure; the endosperm provides nutrition; the cotyledons
% are the site of food storage; the embryo grows into a miniature plant; and
% that embryo differentiates into a radicle (rudimentary root), a hypocotyl
% (stem), and a plumule (bud)." Before you can reason about a seed — identify
% it, germinate it, or teach it — you must first know WHICH part you are looking
% at and what that part is FOR. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? seed_part_role(cotyledon, $Role)      % food_storage
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `seed_part_role(part, role)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the role
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a word
% that is not one of these seed parts (it never invents a role).
%
% Each seed part and the defining role / function the source states for it, as a
% little truth table:
%
%     seed part    defining role / function   a beginner's cue
%     ---------    -----------------------    ------------------------------
%     seed_coat    covering                   the outer jacket that wraps the seed
%     endosperm    nutrition                  the built-in packed lunch
%     cotyledon    food_storage               the seed leaf that holds the food
%     embryo       miniature_plant            the tiny plant-to-be inside
%     radicle      rudimentary_root           the first root of the embryo
%     hypocotyl    stem                       the embryonic stem
%     plumule      bud                         the embryonic shoot / bud
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a role and recall the seed part:
%
%     ? seed_part_role($Part, food_storage)   % cotyledon
%
% A word that is NOT one of these seed parts — `petal`, `stamen`, `sepal` — is
% deliberately NOT a row, so a seed-part recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a role. (The petal is a flower part, but not a part of the SEED.)
% That honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to build a
% reasoner on: it answers exactly the part→role pairs it can ground, and only
% those.
%
% The `role` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a word
% the source EXPLICITLY uses for that part — never one the source does not
% support. Where the source's phrase is more than one word (the endosperm is a
% "source of nutrients"/"provides nutrition"; the cotyledons are the "site of
% food storage"; the radicle is a "rudimentary root"), the atom joins the
% source's own words with underscores, so every value stays literally checkable
% against the quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every part→role pair below
% was read out of a single fetched USDA Forest Service page, and any pair whose
% role could not be verified there was dropped). Each pair, with the verbatim
% span that states it, from "The Woody Plant Seed Manual," Chapter 1: Seed
% Biology (Franklin T. Bonner, USDA Forest Service — a primary U.S. government
% forestry publication):
%   https://www.fs.usda.gov/nsl/Wpsm/Chapter1.pdf
%
%   seed_coat → covering
%     "As endosperm and embryo grow, the surrounding maternal tissues develop
%      into the seed-covering structures, collectively called the seedcoat."
%
%   endosperm → nutrition
%     "Endosperm tissue provides nutrition for the developing embryo, and in
%      some genera ..."
%
%   cotyledon → food_storage
%     "The cotyledons of the embryo become the site of food storage, and these
%      species are designated as nonendospermic."
%
%   embryo → miniature_plant
%     "The embryo grows and differentiates into a miniature plant with radicle
%      (rudimentary root), hypocotyl (stem), plumule (bud), and cotyledons."
%
%   radicle → rudimentary_root
%     "The embryo grows and differentiates into a miniature plant with radicle
%      (rudimentary root), hypocotyl (stem), plumule (bud), and cotyledons."
%
%   hypocotyl → stem
%     "The embryo grows and differentiates into a miniature plant with radicle
%      (rudimentary root), hypocotyl (stem), plumule (bud), and cotyledons."
%
%   plumule → bud
%     "The embryo grows and differentiates into a miniature plant with radicle
%      (rudimentary root), hypocotyl (stem), plumule (bud), and cotyledons."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the USDA statement that fixes the
% first row (seed_coat → covering). Every OTHER row's verbatim span is listed
% above, so each role remains independently checkable. The source is a primary
% U.S. government (USDA Forest Service) seed-biology publication, so `trust
% authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table seed_part_role {
    columns part, role

    row (seed_coat, covering)
    row (endosperm, nutrition)
    row (cotyledon, food_storage)
    row (embryo, miniature_plant)
    row (radicle, rudimentary_root)
    row (hypocotyl, stem)
    row (plumule, bud)

    source "As endosperm and embryo grow, the surrounding maternal tissues develop into the seed-covering structures, collectively called the seedcoat."
    locator "https://www.fs.usda.gov/nsl/Wpsm/Chapter1.pdf"
    trust authoritative
}
